dsh: reasoning:
The new rev12 seems to address the findings. Note interesting: line 72 says whole-curve containment is strictly STRONGER and NOT the predicate; line 213 says visibility is the tail formulation. The predecessor refuted flash-19's HF-06, which claimed whole-curve vs tail differ in the other direction... Actually HF-06 was "whole-curve single-q and tail single-q visibility" — predecessor said equivalent by past-closure of J^-(q); the new text says whole-curve is strictly stronger. Hmm, that's a semantic tension worth checking! Let me think.

Predecessor: "HF-06 (flash-19, critical) refuted: whole-curve single-q and tail single-q visibility are equivalent by past-closure of J^-(q); 241-model exhaustive check 0 divergences, teeth control 26, past-closed control 0".

The new rev12 text (line 72) says: "Whole-curve containment gamma([0,T)) subset J^-(q) is strictly STRONGER and is NOT the predicate of this class [rev12: F1-review-19 HF-06 critical, independently confirmed by worker-037 W037V2-F1 and worker-078 W078-F1-QUANT-ADJ-01]".

Hmm — "strictly stronger" claim contradicts past-closure equivalence? Let's think carefully. Visibility asks: exists q in I+ and t0 in [0,T) such that tail gamma([t0,T)) ⊆ J^-(q) ∩ M. Whole-curve version: gamma([0,T)) ⊆ J^-(q) ∩ M. If gamma is causal and future-directed, then for t0 < t, gamma(t0) ≺ gamma(t) (causally precedes), so if gamma(t) ∈ J^-(q) for all t ≥ t0, then gamma(t0) ∈ J^-(gamma(t)) ⊆ J^-(q) by transitivity of causal past. So tail containment implies whole-curve containment (since for t < t0, gamma(t) ≺ gamma(t0) ∈ J^-(q)). Ah, but careful: J^-(q) is past of q; if the tail is contained in J^-(q), then for any t' < t0, gamma(t') ≺ gamma(t0) and gamma(t0) ∈ J^-(q) → gamma(t') ∈ J^-(q). So tail ⇒ whole. Therefore whole-curve is NOT strictly stronger; it's equivalent (given gamma causal and J^-(q) past-closed). Wait, but the containment also intersects M; J^-(q) ∩ M is past-closed in M presumably (causal past in spacetime M is past-closed under causal curves within M). Yes, for a globally hyperbolic M, J^-(q)∩M is past-closed. So tail ⊆ J^-(q)∩M implies whole curve ⊆ J^-(q)∩M.

But wait: is gamma([0,T)) ⊆ J^-(q) maybe requires gamma(0)∈J^-(q)? With tail at t0, all t<t0 map into past of gamma(t0). Since gamma(t0) ∈ J^-(q), transitivity gives gamma(t) ∈ J^-(q). So yes equivalent, provided gamma is causal (future-directed causal geodesic) and M globally hyperbolic (causal past closed). Unless q ∈ I+ (boundary at infinity), where J^-(q)∩M may not be past-closed? If q ∈ I+ and J^-(q)∩M... hmm, J^-(q) for boundary point q. Standard definition of causal past of a point in the conformal completion: J^-(q) ∩ M. Past-closure of that set in M holds if the conformal completion is causally well-behaved. But that's exactly assumption territory.

So the new rev12 text's claim "strictly STRONGER" is *dubious* — actually the predecessor's instrument found 0 divergences over 241 finite partial orders with past-closure, i.e., equivalence. So rev12 may have introduced a *semantic overclaim* in the opposite direction from HF-06: they now say whole-curve strictly stronger, whereas actually the two are equivalent (for past-closed J^-(q) and causal curve). Wait — but the whole-curve reading vs tail reading: whole-curve containment requires gamma([0,T)) ⊆ J^-(q)∩M which includes gamma(0). Tail containment requires ∃t0. As shown, tail ⇒ whole. Whole ⇒ tail trivially (take t0=0). So they're equivalent. Hence "strictly STRONGER" is false. Unless they mean something else: "whole-curve single-q" might require that the *whole* curve is in the past of some q, whereas the tail requires only a tail. As shown, equivalent for causal curves. Yes.

Hmm, but careful: what if gamma is not contained in M globally... no, gamma: [0,T) → M.

Actually, hold on. Predecessor's proof: "equivalent by past-closure of J^-(q)". Exactly. So rev12's note contradicts the refutation. That's a potential new finding: the rev12 repair overcorrects — it changed the canonical predicate from "whole-curve" to "tail" (semantically equivalent) but asserts "strictly STRONGER", which is mathematically false given past-closure. However, is that a hard finding? The predicate definition itself is coherent (tail is a valid equivalent formulation), the overclaim is a doc/justification error that could mislead (and it's used to justify "canonical"). It's a soft/advisory finding — the fix works and does not change semantics, but the stated strictness is wrong; also it dismisses the earlier whole-curve reading incorrectly.

